"texte": [
"For decades, high-stakes standardized exams and numerical grading systems have served as the primary yardsticks for measuring academic achievement. Proponents of traditional testing argue that objective examinations provide an impartial, transparent benchmark that allows universities and employers to compare candidates fairly across different institutions. Without standardized metrics, they warn, grading could easily succumb to teacher bias or grade inflation.",
"Nevertheless, a growing chorus of educators and psychologists contends that conventional assessment methods offer a remarkably narrow snapshot of human intelligence. High-stakes final exams predominantly measure short-term memory recall and performance under acute time pressure, while largely ignoring essential attributes such as creativity, resilience, and sustained effort. Consequently, many capable students suffer from debilitating test anxiety that prevents them from demonstrating their genuine understanding.",
"Furthermore, numerical grades often shift students' motivation from intrinsic curiosity to extrinsic validation. When learners become obsessed with chasing percentages or letter grades, they tend to avoid intellectually challenging tasks for fear of lowering their average. Conversely, alternative approaches—such as continuous formative assessment, portfolio evaluations, and detailed qualitative feedback—encourage students to view mistakes as constructive stepping stones toward mastery.",
"Ultimately, reforming assessment does not require abolishing all rigorous standards. Rather, if education systems were to adopt a balanced combination of summative exams and continuous, competency-based evaluations, grading would become a genuine tool for learning rather than a mere mechanism for sorting."
],
