"Economists often invoke Joseph Schumpeter's concept of 'creative destruction' to explain this phenomenon. According to this theory, while emerging technologies inevitably destroy outdated jobs, they simultaneously generate entirely new industries and roles—such as cybersecurity analysts, renewable energy technicians, and AI ethicists—that were unimaginable a generation ago.",
"Nevertheless, historical optimism offers scant comfort to displaced workers in the present. Unlike previous industrial revolutions, which unfolded over several decades and allowed generations to adapt gradually, the current digital transition is occurring within a matter of years. Moreover, a fifty-year-old factory supervisor or administrative clerk cannot effortlessly transition into a software engineering role without substantial financial and educational support.",
"Consequently, managing the disappearance of traditional professions requires proactive social policies rather than passive faith in market forces. Unless governments and corporations invest heavily in vocational retraining programs and transitional income support, technological progress risks leaving millions of experienced workers permanently marginalized."
