"texte": [
"When social media platforms first emerged, they were widely hailed as revolutionary instruments of democratic empowerment. By dismantling the traditional gatekeeping monopoly held by major newspapers and television broadcasters, digital networks enabled ordinary citizens, whistleblowers, and marginalized activists to broadcast their voices instantaneously to a global audience. In authoritarian contexts, social networks have repeatedly served as vital tools for organizing peaceful protests and exposing human rights abuses.",
"However, this unprecedented democratization of speech has revealed a dark, destabilizing corollary: the viral proliferation of misinformation. Unlike reputable journalism, which relies on editorial verification and legal accountability, social media platforms operate on engagement-driven algorithms. Empirical studies consistently show that emotionally charged falsehoods, outrage-inducing headlines, and conspiracy theories spread significantly faster and further than nuanced, factual reporting.",
"Consequently, platforms face an acute dilemma regarding content moderation. When technology companies aggressively remove misleading posts or ban accounts, critics accuse them of exercising unaccountable corporate censorship over public discourse. Conversely, when platforms adopt a laissez-faire approach under the banner of absolute free speech, hate speech and medical or electoral disinformation flourish unchecked.",
"Ultimately, protecting freedom of expression does not require granting algorithms the power to amplify falsehoods for profit. If democratic societies distinguish between the right to speak and the right to algorithmic amplification, platforms can preserve open public debate while curbing the toxic spread of engineered deception."
],
