"texte": [
"At first glance, modern digital technologies appear to have bestowed unprecedented autonomy upon the individual. Armed with a single smartphone, a person can navigate unfamiliar cities without consulting a paper map, translate foreign languages instantaneously, manage bank accounts without visiting a branch, and acquire new academic skills through free online courses. For individuals with physical disabilities, assistive technologies—such as voice-controlled smart homes and screen readers—have dismantled longstanding barriers to independent living.",
"Yet, beneath this surface of effortless empowerment lies a paradoxical form of technological dependency. As we increasingly outsource routine cognitive tasks to digital assistants, fundamental human faculties begin to atrophy. Neuroscientists refer to 'digital amnesia' to describe how reliance on search engines weakens memory retention, while constant dependence on GPS navigation impairs spatial awareness and mental mapping skills.",
"Furthermore, hyper-reliance on interconnected digital infrastructure exposes individuals to acute vulnerability whenever systems fail. A prolonged power outage, a cyberattack, or a software malfunction can abruptly render smart homes uninhabitable, digital payments impossible, and cloud-stored documents inaccessible. In other words, we have traded self-sufficient practical competence for fragile convenience.",
"Ultimately, technology enhances human independence only when it serves as a tool that augments our capabilities rather than a crutch that replaces them. To remain genuinely autonomous, individuals must consciously preserve basic offline skills while utilizing digital innovations selectively."
