"Following his presentation, geriatric nurse practitioner Clara Alvarez offered a compelling ethical counterpoint during the panel discussion. 'Dr. Sato has brilliantly demonstrated what robotics can achieve in technical environments,' she acknowledged. 'However, in fields like nursing, psychotherapy, and early-childhood education, efficiency is not the primary metric of success. When an elderly dementia patient is terrified at night, they do not need an automated voice module; they need the reassuring warmth of a human hand and genuine emotional empathy.'",
"Dr. Sato nodded respectfully, agreeing that physical dexterity is fundamentally distinct from emotional intelligence. 'Indeed, simulating empathy through pre-programmed phrases can actually feel deceptive and alienating to vulnerable individuals,' he noted. 'Even though companion robots may assist with reminding patients to take medication, they cannot replicate authentic human solidarity.'",
"Ultimately, the symposium participants reached a clear consensus: robotics should be deployed to replace humans in dangerous or hyper-repetitive tasks, while serving strictly as supportive assistants in care-centered professions where moral judgment and human connection remain irreplaceable."
