"When synthetic plastics were mass-produced in the mid-twentieth century, they were celebrated as miracle materials—lightweight, durable, sterile, and inexpensive. Today, however, that very durability has transformed plastic into an omnipresent ecological nightmare. Over four hundred million tons of plastic are produced globally each year, nearly half of which is designed for single-use packaging discarded within minutes of purchase. Because conventional plastics take centuries to decompose, millions of tons accumulate annually in landfills, rivers, and oceans.",
"The consequences for marine and terrestrial ecosystems are devastating. Sea turtles, seabirds, and whales frequently mistake floating plastic bags for jellyfish or become fatally entangled in discarded fishing nets. Worse still, sunlight and ocean waves gradually fragment larger plastic debris into microscopic particles known as microplastics. These toxic fragments have infiltrated the entire global food web, appearing in table salt, bottled water, agricultural soils, and even human bloodstreams and placental tissue.",
"For decades, the plastics industry promoted household recycling as the primary solution to plastic waste. Yet, global data reveals that fewer than ten percent of all plastic ever produced has actually been recycled, due to technical contamination and low economic profitability.",
