"texte": [
"Medical progress has always required venturing into uncharted scientific territory, yet history offers grim reminders of what transpires when scientific ambition operates unchecked by moral boundaries. Following egregious twentieth-century abuses—where vulnerable patients were subjected to experimental procedures without their knowledge—modern bioethics established strict foundational principles: informed consent, non-maleficence, and independent ethical review boards.",
"Today, however, exponential advances in biotechnology are testing those established frameworks in unprecedented ways. CRISPR gene-editing tools now allow scientists to alter human DNA with remarkable precision. While somatic gene therapy—which treats genetic diseases in an individual patient without passing changes to offspring—is widely celebrated, germline editing introduces heritable modifications into human embryos. Critics warn that crossing this threshold risks opening the door to non-medical 'designer babies,' deepening social inequality and reviving eugenic ideologies.",
"Similarly, clinical trials conducted by multinational pharmaceutical companies in developing nations raise pressing ethical questions. When impoverished populations participate in drug trials because they lack access to basic healthcare, genuine voluntary consent becomes compromised, especially if the resulting medications remain unaffordable in those very communities.",
"Ultimately, ethical limits are not obstacles designed to obstruct scientific discovery; rather, they are essential guardrails that protect human rights. Science must continually ask not only 'Can we perform this experiment?' but also 'Should we?'"
],
