"Across the table, legal scholar Professor David Thorne nodded in agreement before adding another dimension to the debate. 'Equally troubling are the ethical dilemmas surrounding predictive genetic testing and artificial reproductive technologies,' he noted. 'When prospective parents can screen embryos for dozens of polygenic traits—ranging from disease susceptibility to height or cognitive predispositions—where do we draw the line between therapeutic medicine and human enhancement?'",
"'Furthermore, the integration of predictive algorithms into hospital triage raises profound questions of accountability,' Dr. Rostova continued. 'If an AI diagnostic tool recommends withholding scarce intensive-care beds from elderly or disabled patients based on statistical survival probabilities, who bears moral and legal responsibility for that decision?'",
"Concluding their session, the committee emphasized that medical innovation outpaces statutory law at an accelerating rate. Unless societies establish inclusive, multidisciplinary ethics councils—bringing together physicians, philosophers, patients, and lawmakers—technological capability risks dictating moral values rather than serving them."
