"Responding on the same broadcast, constitutional lawyer Marcus Vance offered a more nuanced legal perspective. 'Freedom of artistic expression is indeed a cornerstone of democracy, Sophia,' he agreed. 'However, like all fundamental rights, it does not exist in a vacuum. Democratic legal systems rightly establish narrow boundaries where artistic expression crosses into incitement to hatred, defamation, or direct physical harm to vulnerable individuals.'",
"'Those legal thresholds are understandable, Marcus, yet my greater concern today is informal censorship,' Sophia replied. 'Increasingly, museums and publishers practice self-censorship out of fear of online outrage mobs or losing corporate sponsors, effectively silencing bold, dissenting voices before the public can even judge the work.'",
"Ultimately, navigating this tension requires distinguishing between legal prohibition and critical debate. In an open society, citizens retain every right to criticize, boycott, or protest an artwork they find offensive, provided state authorities and mob intimidation do not suppress the artist's right to create."
],
