"texte": [
"Prejudice rarely stems from direct personal experience; more often, it flourishes in the vacuum of unfamiliarity, fed by historical caricatures and sensationalist media narratives. In the 1950s, psychologist Gordon Allport formulated the 'Contact Hypothesis,' proposing that sustained, cooperative interaction between members of different cultural or social groups significantly reduces mutual hostility and stereotyping.",
"Organized cultural exchanges—such as youth twinning programs, international artist residencies, and collaborative university workshops—put this psychological insight into practice. When individuals work side by side toward a shared creative or civic goal, abstract labels dissolve into individual human relationships. Discovering shared humor, parallel family anxieties, and common aspirations humanizes 'the other' in ways that abstract tolerance lectures never could.",
"Nevertheless, cross-cultural researchers emphasize that superficial encounters do not automatically dismantle prejudice. If exchanges reinforce unequal power dynamics—such as wealthy volunteers briefly visiting impoverished communities without genuine partnership—they can inadvertently entrench paternalistic attitudes.",
"Therefore, effective cultural exchanges must be built on strict reciprocity, equal status, and sustained dialogue. When structured around mutual learning rather than one-sided curiosity, cultural exchanges become powerful laboratories for empathy and lasting peace."
],
