"First among these duties is informed participation. Casting a ballot is meaningless—or even perilous—if voters rely solely on partisan slogans or viral misinformation rather than scrutinizing candidates' policies and track records. Furthermore, democratic citizenship requires respecting the rule of law, paying taxes equitably to fund shared public goods, and serving on juries when called upon.",
"Equally vital is the cultivation of democratic tolerance and civil discourse. In pluralistic societies, citizens inevitably hold deeply conflicting religious, moral, and economic beliefs. Democracy does not demand unanimity, but it does require viewing political opponents as fellow citizens with legitimate voices rather than existential enemies to be silenced.",
"Ultimately, democracy is not a spectator sport where citizens passively consume governance every few years. By staying informed, engaging respectfully across ideological divides, and holding institutions accountable between elections, ordinary citizens fulfill the indispensable duties that keep freedom alive."
