"texte": [
"In large-scale democracies, corporations, and online communities, individuals frequently succumb to a psychological phenomenon known as the 'diffusion of responsibility.' When millions of people participate in an election, a market economy, or an environmental crisis, it is tempting to assume that one single voice, vote, or ethical choice is statistically insignificant. Consequently, citizens often rationalize passivity by telling themselves that collective outcomes depend entirely on powerful leaders or distant institutions.",
"However, history and social science repeatedly demonstrate that collective decisions are nothing more than the cumulative sum of individual choices. Every major democratic shift, consumer boycott, or ethical workplace reform began with solitary individuals refusing to hide behind crowd anonymity. Conversely, when ordinary citizens abdicate moral responsibility—whether by spreading unverified rumors online, ignoring workplace misconduct, or abstaining from local elections—they actively enable collective dysfunction.",
"Furthermore, organizational psychologists warn against 'groupthink,' a dynamic in which team members suppress dissenting opinions to preserve superficial harmony, leading institutions into catastrophic decisions. A single courageous individual willing to ask uncomfortable ethical questions can break unanimous conformity and steer a group toward wiser judgment.",
"Ultimately, belonging to a collective never absolves the individual of moral agency. When each citizen recognizes that their daily actions, votes, and words either strengthen or weaken the common good, individual responsibility becomes the indispensable cornerstone of healthy collective life."
